[center][size=6][b]Heart of Magic Librarian[/b][/size]
[size=3]Spell learning as practice, on a tree grown from your own load order[/size]

A rebuild of [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/171146]Heart of Magic - Spell Learning System[/url] by Dinkel Zombie,
released with the original author's permission to improve and re-release it.[/center]
[center][color=#ffff00][size=6]PLZ read change log. [/size]
[/color][/center]
[center]Source code: [url=https://github.com/Iwillbewarlock/HeartOfMagic/tree/v1.6.0]https://github.com/Iwillbewarlock/HeartOfMagic/tree/v1.6.0[/url][/center]
[center][youtube]fyMM1G6eWow[/youtube]

[img]{{IMG_02}}[/img][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]What this mod is[/b][/size]

Spells are no longer learned the moment you read a tome. Heart of Magic Librarian scans every spell in your load order - vanilla and mods alike - and grows a skill tree for each school, where each spell leads to related, stronger ones. You learn a spell by working toward it: pick it as your goal, cast the spells on the road to it, study its tome, practise the school. Early on you can cast it weakened; at mastery it reaches full power.

No spell lists to maintain and no patches per spell mod: the tree is built from what is installed. Open the panel (F8), scan, build - and the College is in session.

[center][img]{{GIF_SCAN}}[/img][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]New in 1.6.0[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Paths of Empyrean support:[/b] Spells from other spell mods now get the Lost Grimoire poison, sun, disease and kinetic keywords and the Paths of Empyrean daedra summon keyword that its perks look for, so those perks work on them too.
[*][b]Environment mods support:[/b] Fire, frost and shock damage effects of spell mods get the keywords that Rain Extinguishes Fires, Water Douses Fire, Sneak Tools, DISDD, Elementally Reactive Waters, Ice is Slick and SIM look for. They also get the own keywords of the OIF rule packs (Dynamic Torches NG, Fires Off, Melt The Ice, Burn The Hay and the rest), Dynamic Candles, Put Out The Fires and Frostwalker's frost and fire cloaks. Keywords are added only where the spell's record says the element, not from its name. You can turn this off with [b]"environment": { "enabled": false }[/b] in config.json.
[*][b]Element detection and balance:[/b] A spell's element now follows what its own record says when a keyword framework guesses otherwise. Desecration's necrotic flames (plain magic damage) and Flames of Coldharbour's cold fire (frost) no longer count as fire because their names contain "fire", and they leave the fire group of the tree (a tree built before keeps its layout until you Scan, Build and Apply again). For balance, when Rain Extinguishes Fires, Water Douses Fire, Sneak Tools, DISDD, Elementally Reactive Waters, SIM or Ice is Slick is installed, the vanilla element keywords go on every effect whose record deals that damage, not only the first. Because perk overhauls (Adamant, Ordinator, Mysticism and others) read the same keywords, their element perks apply to the extra effects too. Without one of those mods, nothing changes.
[*][b]New plugin and Frostwalker + Mysticism fix:[/b] Added a required light (ESL) plugin, [b]HeartOfMagic.esp[/b] (one record and only Skyrim.esm as master), and an optional Mysticism patch, [b]HeartOfMagic_Mysticism.esp[/b], offered by the installer when Mysticism is active. Load HeartOfMagic_Mysticism.esp after HeartOfMagic.esp and after any other plugin that edits Mysticism's screen-shake effect. With Frostwalker and Mysticism, Fire Storm, Hysteria, Harmony and Mysticism's other master spells no longer freeze water (Mysticism's hidden screen-shake effect placed the vanilla Blizzard explosion, which Frostwalker reads as frost; HeartOfMagic.esp holds a neutral copy of that explosion). Note: Blizzard itself does not freeze water with this fix, because its storm is a hazard effect Frostwalker does not react to (it only froze through the borrowed explosion).
[*][b]Settings:[/b] Split into four categories - [b]Display[/b], [b]Learning[/b], [b]Spell Tomes[/b] and [b]Advanced[/b] (developer and debug options are only there) - picked from a slim list at the left, and each keeps its scroll position while the game runs. Rows are no longer boxed in bands and underlines, and the version moved to the foot of the Settings page. [b]Reset to Defaults[/b] (and the power steps reset) now asks for a second click. In Settings > Spell Tome Learning, the message now says "1 spell added" instead of "1 spells added" (with correct Polish plurals).
[*][b]Scan tab:[/b] One line says where you are ("753 spells found · 681 go in the tree · not applied yet", with what was left out and why), and one main button follows the step: [b]Scan Spells[/b], [b]Build Tree[/b], [b]Apply Tree[/b]. [b]Scan again[/b], [b]Import[/b] (moved here from the tree footer) and [b]Clear draft tree[/b] are small buttons beside it. Per-school counts fold under "Scan details", excluded spells and the plugin list read as lines with their state, and the unfinished Alternate Pathways tab is hidden.
[*][b]Spell card:[/b] Built to answer "can I learn this now". One line shows the state, XP share and, for a weakened spell, the power it casts at ("Weakened · 62% learned · casts at 50% power" - fixed so "Weakened" and the casting power show for a spell learned early and not yet mastered). "Still needed" lists only what is missing (required spells, optional ones with how many more are needed, e.g. "need 1 of 2", and locks from another school marked as such), switching to "Requirements met" once nothing is left. It also shows why [b]Learn[/b] cannot be pressed yet and folds the spells it leads to into one line. Optional requirement counts, the edit mode box to change that number, and the [b]LOCK[/b] badge are back.
[*][b]Tree view and import fixes:[/b] The tree now has one thin frame, tools floating on the canvas, schools as small coloured labels, a one-line footer that shows the spell count once, and a "?" button that opens the colour key. In Arcane, Night Grimoire, Chalkboard and Skald, the corner drawings are smaller. Texts were checked for contrast in all six designs and for long German and Russian words and small screens (1280x720). A tree imported or edited by hand with formIds written without "0x" no longer mixes spells up, and text that is not a formId is skipped with a line in SpellLearning.log instead of stopping the request.
[/list]
[b]After updating:[/b] enable [b]HeartOfMagic.esp[/b] in your mod manager (and the Mysticism patch, [b]HeartOfMagic_Mysticism.esp[/b], if offered - load it after HeartOfMagic.esp and after any other plugin that edits Mysticism's screen-shake effect). Your saved tree and saves keep working. Spells whose element changed move to their new group only after Scan, Build and Apply.

[spoiler]

[size=5][b]New in 1.5.1[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]New design - Skald[/b] (Settings > UI Display > Design). It looks like Skyrim's own menus: smoky black background, thin pale lines, woven knotwork in the page corners, wide-set capital titles, and the magic schools' colours softened to quiet tints. It uses the Bahnschrift font that comes with Windows 10 and 11; without it, the panel's usual narrow font is used.
[*][b]Saved tree and load order changes:[/b] when a light (ESL) plugin is removed or moved, a saved tree's requirements stay with their own spells instead of ending up on the wrong ones. A spell from a plugin that is no longer active, or from a plugin made ESL after the tree was built, is no longer swapped for an unrelated spell. The spell gate's [b]Include[/b] choice is also kept for plugins whose file names use non-Latin letters (Korean, Japanese, Chinese) or accented capitals; older choices are converted once, automatically.
[*][b]XP caps, tome XP and passive learning:[/b] the per-spell XP caps (casting other spells, same-school spells, direct prerequisites) now carry over through save and load instead of opening again on every load. Tome XP is given once per tome per save: loading an earlier save lets you get it again, and restarting the game no longer gives it a second time. Passive learning now reaches its tier limits (it stopped at 25%).
[*][b]ISL and other mods' XP sources:[/b] with the ISL patch, swapping tomes while studying no longer adds study XP past the threshold, and a spell is not counted as mastered before ISL has taught it to you. Settings for other mods' XP sources (for example Book Reading turned off, or its multiplier lowered) apply from the start of the game. The BookXP add-on's corner message now shows the XP your spells really got; it always said +15, so lowering its multiplier looked like it did nothing.
[*][b]Settings:[/b] the [b]XP Caps[/b] sliders keep their values between sessions (they went back to 5 / 15 / 50), and [b]Reset to Defaults[/b] resets them too. A setting changed just before closing the panel is saved. If config.json cannot be read completely, the panel no longer saves over it (your blacklist, whitelist and chosen roots stay), and the Scan tab's status line says so.
[*][b]Spell text in the game's menus:[/b] changing your learning target no longer leaves the "[x% Power]" note on another spell that shares an effect. With [b]Modify Game Display[/b] off, spell names and descriptions are left unchanged, and turning it off puts them back.
[*][b]Tree:[/b] after [b]Unlock[/b] (and the cheat-mode Unlock), the tree is redrawn at once and the next spells open. The Classic tier zone chart shows the current values after a preset is applied, and dragging it uses them. In edit mode, requirement changes take effect when you leave edit mode (before, only after a restart), and spells added there keep their place after a load order change.
[*][b]For mod authors:[/b] SetSpellXP is limited to 0-100%. Another mod's XP source cannot use the names Heart of Magic uses itself (such as "passive"), and very long source names are refused.
[/list]
[b]After updating:[/b] nothing to do - your saved tree and saves keep working, no rebuild needed. On a save from 1.5.0 the XP caps start counting from zero once. Saves made with 1.5.1 still load in 1.5.0 with all progress; only the new cap counters are lost there.

[size=5][b]New in 1.5.0[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]The spell tree builds itself on the first start.[/b] With no saved tree, the game scans your spells and builds the tree at the main menu with your active scanner preset - no Scan and Build needed. If you load a save first, the rest waits until you open the panel (which pauses the game) or return to the main menu, so play is never interrupted. A tree you already have is never rebuilt; if your load order changed since it was built, the Scan tab and a one-time corner message tell you. Settings > Hotkey > [b]Build Spell Tree at Start[/b]: [i]When none[/i] (default) or [i]Off[/i].
[*][b]Hand-drawn icons[/b] in place of the bracket glyphs, in each design's own style (line, ink, gold ink, chalk), and a tidier panel. Icon shapes from game-icons.net (CC BY 3.0).
[*][b]Frozen Electrocuted Combustion works with spell mods:[/b] sun-tagged spells get FEC's sun death, air damage spells its suffocation (where a mod defines MagicDamageWind). Scrolls and staffs that share the damage effect get the same death. In memory only, with FEC.esp loaded.
[*][b]Perk and FEC keywords stay on:[/b] one that something else removes is put back at new game and save load.
[*][b]A button in Risa's All In One Menu[/b] (5.4 or newer).
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] adding a plugin in the middle of the load order no longer mixes up chosen roots, Pre Req Master locks or custom XP (custom XP set before 1.5.0: set it again if your load order changes); an empty tree is never saved over your file; the right version in the panel's corner; the tree opens centred above the spell card bar; the Scan page's preview fits its box; chapter titles stay off the spells; a language change updates every label at once.
[/list]
[b]After updating:[/b] nothing to do - your saved tree keeps working.


[size=5][b]New in 1.4.0[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Fixes from your reports[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]A tome that teaches a power, lesser power or ability teaches it at full strength at once[/b] (a mod's teleport power, for one, was learned at 20% and did not work). Such spells are never in the tree, so their tomes work the vanilla way now, also with the ISL patch. A save that already holds one learned weakened gets it back at full strength with its own name when it loads. The same goes for a tome spell with no magic school.
[*][b]Less work behind the game while the panel is closed[/b] (a report of lower FPS with it closed), and [b]scanning freezes the game for much less time[/b]: the scan's text work and classification run in the background.
[*]A spell that only a mod's [b]debug tome[/b] teaches (a test cell reached by console) stays out of the tree.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Perks and tags[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]More perk trees work with every spell mod:[/b] Hydromancy, Atromancy, Hemomancy, Venomancy and Everdamned give their perks only to the spells their own patches name; Heart of Magic now adds their keywords to every spell with that element, whatever mod it comes from. Subclasses of Skyrim 2 turns on the Adamant and Ascension adapters too.
[*][b]Cost perks reach more modded spells:[/b] a tome spell whose plugin left its half-cost perk empty never got cheaper with the Novice/Apprentice/... perks. It now gets the vanilla one for its school and tier (in memory only; the tree still reads the spell as its plugin wrote it).
[*][b]Better spell tags, so the tree groups more spells together:[/b] stagger and knockback spells are force; modded poison, sun, necrotic, disease and wind damage are recognised; speed, carry weight and skill buffs are strengthen or curse; mod teleports are teleport. Absorbs are no longer curses, daedra summons no longer soul magic, paralysis no longer creature or human magic, and summons, reanimations and blood-named defences no longer blood magic.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Panel[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]No hotkey, if you like:[/b] Settings > Hotkey has a [b]None[/b] button (to get a key back: console [i]cgf "SpellLearning.ToggleMenu"[/i], or [i]hotkeyCode[/i] in config.json). Picking a letter key warns that typing it in a search box closes the panel.
[*][b]Smoother:[/b] zoomed out (the opening view included) at most 50 spell names are drawn, without outline - the selected and learning ones always, the rest nearest the middle of the screen; the names' letters are prepared while the panel sits still, so the first zoom-in stalls much less (most noticeable with Korean, Chinese or Japanese names); the idle panel repaints about half as much.
[/list]
[b]After updating:[/b] open the panel, [b]Scan[/b], and [b]build the tree again[/b].

[size=5][b]New in 1.3.0[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Spell Research compatibility[/b][/size]
With [b]Spell Research[/b] loaded, the spells your mods' tomes teach that Spell Research does not know yet are added to its research lists - after its Import and at every save load - so they turn up in research, spell composing and tome copying. No patch per spell mod.
[list]
[*][b]The same spells as your tree:[/b] the same tome checks, your blacklist, the plugins you turned off and the spell gate below. Each spell once, however many mods carry it.
[*]A corner message says how many spells were added. [i]"spellResearch": { "enabled": false }[/i] in [i]config.json[/i] turns it off.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Only the player's spells in the tree[/b][/size]
A spell gate keeps out what a tome teaches but a player never casts as magic.
[list]
[*][b]Left out by itself:[/b] spells that cost nothing to cast - pet, mount and follower calls, a mod's utility spells - and spells that only dismiss a summon.
[*][b]Spells to look over:[/b] summons that stay for hours, horse summons, a summon of one named character, cheap script-only spells. They stay in the tree, listed with the reason in the Blacklist window: [i]Leave out[/i] or [i]Keep[/i].
[*]Got one wrong? [i]Include[/i] puts a left-out spell back - into the tree and into Spell Research.
[/list]

[size=4][b]A better tree[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Type grouping[/b] (asked for in the Posts): bound weapons grow from bound weapons, runes from runes, and fire, frost and shock stay on their own branches. Set its strength on the scan page; 0 builds as 1.2.0 did.
[*][b]Tiers by Magicka cost[/b] (asked for in the Posts): an option to order each school's tiers by cost instead of Novice to Master.
[*][b]One spell, once:[/b] when two mods carry the same spell (Saints and Seducers and ECSS, for one), the tree has it once, and either tome teaches it. Needs powerofthree's Tweaks.
[*][b]Editing prerequisites[/b] in edit mode works as shown again (a new link no longer turns up as both Required and Optional).
[/list]

[size=4][b]In your language, easier to read[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]The in-game messages (learned, mastered, power increased, tome study...) and every text in the panel follow the panel's language - 13 languages - and change at once when you switch.
[*][b]XP Alerts[/b] switch: turn off the XP and power messages; learned and mastered always show.
[*]Bigger text by default, Font Size up to 2.0x, a resizable tool bar, Find Spell as a dropdown, and no more stutter when zooming over a spell.
[/list]
[b]After updating:[/b] open the panel, [b]Scan[/b], and [b]build the tree again[/b]. With Spell Research, press its [i]Import Spells[/i] once.

[size=5][b]New in 1.2.0[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Perk overhauls understand your spell mods[/b][/size]
Perk mods find spells by the keywords on their effects - a fire perk looks for the fire keyword - and a spell mod that left those out got no perk bonuses unless someone made a patch for that exact pair of mods. Librarian now adds the keywords itself, to the spells it has tagged: in memory at game start and after each scan, with no plugin, no patch and nothing written to your save.
[list]
[*][b]Covered:[/b] the vanilla keywords every perk overhaul reads, Adamant and Mysticism, Path of Sorcery, Ascension (1, 2 and the Adamant 6 patch) and Paragon. Vokrii and Ordinator need nothing beyond the vanilla keywords.
[*][b]Careful by design:[/b] a keyword goes on only when the librarian's tag and the effect's own record agree, never on an effect that another spell shares unless that spell qualifies too.
[*][b]Spell lists too:[/b] where a perk asks a list of spells rather than a keyword (Adamant, Vokrii), fitting modded spells are added to that list at the next game start.
[*][b]Your call:[/b] [i]perk_adapters_report.json[/i] lists everything added, and [i]"perkAdapters": { "enabled": false }[/i] in [i]config.json[/i] turns it off.
[/list]

[size=4][b]A tree of spells you can actually find[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]The tree is built from spells a tome teaches - as the old "Tomes only" switch did, which is now simply how it works. Quest, perk and script spells stay out of the tree but still get the perk keywords.
[*]Spells whose tome [b]nothing in your load order hands out[/b] - never placed, sold, dropped, crafted, or given by a quest, a script or a distribution mod (SPID, KID, FLM, SkyPatcher...) - stay out too: you could never find the tome. Librarian reads your plugin files once in the background after the game starts (a few seconds; a scan in that time waits for it). On a 3,900-plugin load order this took out 46 spells from 11 mods.
[*]Mods' script and animation spells that sit in the voice slot stay out of the tree, and their tome teaches them the vanilla way, so none of them takes your shout's place.
[*]Spells from the same mod now grow together in the tree.
[*]A spell's tier now follows its half-cost perk (Novice to Master), as intended - before, a Master spell could land at the root as Novice.
[/list]

[size=4][b]And[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Press the level on a spell card to light every spell of that level, in every school.
[*]Better tags: summons by what they summon, spells by the targets they only work on, vampiric magic as blood, walls and storms as constructs, and many smaller fixes - similar spells group better.
[*]Esc closes the panel again in game.
[/list]
[b]After updating:[/b] open the panel, [b]Scan[/b], and [b]build the tree again[/b] - tags, tiers and the spells in the tree have all changed.
[/spoiler]

[line]

[size=5][b]The original: Heart of Magic[/b][/size]

[url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/171146]Heart of Magic[/url] by Dinkel Zombie turned spell learning into practice: it scanned every spell in the load order, generated prerequisite trees with text analysis (TF-IDF and fuzzy matching over names, effects and descriptions), and tracked XP from casting and tome study. It brought early, weakened casting that grows to full power, Discovery Mode, progression presets, a plugin whitelist and spell blacklist, and a spell tome hook based on Don't Eat Spell Tomes. Later versions ran without Python ("depythoned") and offered several layout and build modes, including an optional LLM builder.

Dinkel Zombie opened the source and gave everyone permission to improve and re-release it. Librarian is that: the same idea, rebuilt around how it feels to use.

[line]

[size=5][b]What changed from Heart of Magic 2.5[/b][/size]

The tree is easier to read, quicker to use and smoother to handle - and it keeps your progress honest.

[size=4][b]Less grinding, more sense[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Known Higher Spells.[/b] Already know a higher spell (bought, given, learned from another mod)? The spells below it open for a share of their XP (30% at Novice up to 80% at Master) instead of making you grind spells you have outgrown. Optionally the whole chain down to the root. On by default, tunable in Settings > Progression.
[*][b]Your cast always counts.[/b] Casting a spell that leads to your current target is always direct XP, whoever set the target - a tome, Papyrus or another mod.
[*][b]Trees that make sense in any language.[/b] Spells of one element grow on one branch, and a Korean, Chinese or Russian game gets the same tree as an English one.
[*][b]Paths between schools.[/b] A fire spell and a fire summon are linked across schools - an extra way in, never an extra requirement.
[/list]

[center][img]{{IMG_04}}[/img][/center]

[size=4][b]Everything you need, at a glance[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]A new spell card:[/b] icon, keyword chips, a description with the real numbers filled in, what it needs and what it opens, and how close you are to mastery.
[*][b]Keyword chips:[/b] click a chip and every spell with that keyword lights up, across schools.
[*][b]Preview on Hover:[/b] rest the cursor on a spell to read its card without clicking.
[*][b]Two spells with the same name[/b] (a mod's copy of a vanilla spell) show their plugin in the card's lists, so you always know which one is meant.
[*][b]Find Spell, school tabs and a Home button[/b] take you anywhere in the tree in one step.
[*][b]A language picker[/b] in Settings (13 languages, every text translated) - no more editing files, and the panel's text switches right away.
[/list]

[center][img]{{IMG_03}}[/img]

[img]{{GIF_CHIPS}}[/img][/center]

[size=4][b]A panel that feels good to handle[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Smooth.[/b] The tree is drawn once and reused: dragging draws only the newly uncovered edge, big repaints are spread over frames, hovering and XP gains no longer redraw the tree, and animations rest when you do. The goal is no visible frame drop in game.
[*][b]A camera that follows you.[/b] Clicking a spell glides to it (Center on Click, Zoom on Click, Focus Zoom, Rotate Wheel, Dim Others - each a setting).
[*][b]Clicks do what you meant.[/b] A click no longer nudges the tree. Esc first drops the selection, then closes the panel; Esc in Find Spell closes only the search.
[*][b]Tidy trees.[/b] Spells are moved off each other, off the lines and off the heart when a tree is built.
[*][b]Six designs[/b] - Classic (the old look), Modern Dark, Arcane (a parchment spellbook), Night Grimoire, Chalkboard and Skald (after Skyrim's own menus) - each with its own lines and fonts. Add-on designs are one JSON file.
[*][b]The Render popup[/b] turns off any moving part with one chip, or everything with "Still everything".
[/list]

[center][img]{{GIF_DESIGNS}}[/img]

[img]{{IMG_06}}[/img]

[img]{{GIF_LANG}}[/img][/center]

[size=4][b]Progress you can trust[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Tree prerequisites were forgotten after every save load in 2.5, so any spell could be learned from a tome for the rest of that session. Fixed.
[*]Early-learned (weakened) spells went back to full power on every load. Fixed.
[*]Spells no longer drop out of the tree after a load-order change.
[*]A possible crash when a cast finished learning a spell, fixed; progress is only changed on the game thread.
[*]The tree and settings are written safely with a backup copy, and saving settings no longer stalls the game.
[*]Simpler: one tree builder (Classic), no LLM or API key, no dead SkyrimNet scripts.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Scanning and tagging, built for patches[/b][/size]
In 2.5 the scan only gathered spells and the tree grouped them by name. In Librarian every scan also runs the [b]Librarian[/b], which gives each spell a set of tags and writes them to [i]spell_catalog.json[/i]. The tags are chosen from the game's own data - what the spell and its magic effects actually are in your game - not from spell names or a fixed list:
[list]
[*][b]Four axes read from the records:[/b] school, tier, casting type and targeting.
[*][b]Elements and techniques from rules:[/b] fire, frost, shock, poison, arcane, blood, water, air, earth, holy, shadow... and summoning, siphon, curse, cloak, dispel... A rule reads what every load order has - magic effect keywords, archetypes, actor values, resistances - so no spell mod needs its own patch.
[*][b]Rules are data, not code.[/b] They are JSON files in [i]SKSE/Plugins/SpellLearning/librarian/[/i], merged by file name. Keyword frameworks (ADAR, KIT, NSV, OCF) get their own rule files that switch on when the framework is installed. Anyone can add a [i]90_user.json[/i] - by effect keyword, plugin name, archetype, or a single spell - without touching the DLL, and a tag outside the known vocabulary is refused with a warning instead of breaking the tree.
[*][b]Load-order-safe IDs.[/b] Spells are keyed by plugin and local ID ([i]Plugin.esp|0x000800[/i]), so a tree and its catalog survive a load-order change.
[*][b]More from the scan:[/b] which spells vampires cast (from NPCs, races, leveled lists and SPID distributions), editor IDs when powerofthree's Tweaks is installed, and a Papyrus call ([i]SpellLearning.RunScan()[/i]) that writes a tagged scan for other tools.
[/list]
The catalog is there for more than this tree: a perk mod, a patch or a spell browser can read the same tags instead of classifying spells again, and new spell mods can be supported with a small rule file.

[b]One honest limit:[/b] magic that is elemental only in concept - blood, water, wind and the like - usually has nothing in its records to say so. It deals plain health damage through a script, with no element keyword and no resistance. Those spells could not be caught by general rules and needed a separate hand-made patch, [i]80_manual.json[/i], which names them spell by spell for the spell mods it was made with. A spell mod of that kind that is not in it will show those spells without an element until someone adds a rule for it.

[b]Want a spell mod supported?[/b] Post its name in the comments or the Posts tab (a link and the spells that look wrong help), and I will add rules for it in an update.

The full list of changes is in the Changelog.

[line]

[size=5][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

[b]Requirements[/b]
[list]
[*]Skyrim Special Edition 1.5.97, Anniversary Edition 1.6.x, or Anniversary Edition 1.7.99 / 1.7.104 - one download for all of them (not VR). 1.7 support is built and checked offline only; it has not been run in game yet.
[*][url=https://skse.silverlock.org/]SKSE64[/url] (the build for your game version)
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/32444]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/url] (on 1.7.x, a version that includes your game version)
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/148718]Prisma UI[/url]
[/list]
One light plugin (HeartOfMagic.esp, ESL-flagged, one record, Skyrim.esm as its only master); no Python, no API key. Safe to add to an existing save.

[b]Works with[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Any spell mod.[/b] Nothing is hard-coded per mod; the tree and the tags come from what is installed. Vanilla, Creation Club and mod spells all go in one tree.
[*][b]Any game language.[/b] Grouping reads scan data and editor IDs before names, and the panel has fonts for Latin, Cyrillic, Korean, Japanese and Chinese.
[*][b]ISL-DESTified / Don't Eat Spell Tomes, I Just Want to Sit Down and Read, Gate to Sovngarde CE (Bookworm trait)[/b] - the installer offers a merged compatibility patch (and picks it by itself when it finds ISL or DEST). Load Heart of Magic Librarian after them.
[*][b]Keyword frameworks[/b] (ADAR, KIT, NSV, OCF) - their tagging rules switch on by themselves.
[*][b]Spell Research[/b] - your mods' tome spells join its research lists, the same ones your tree has; no patch.
[*][b]Perk overhauls[/b] - vanilla perks, Adamant, Mysticism, Path of Sorcery, Ascension, Paragon, Vokrii, Ordinator: modded spells get the keywords they read, with no patch.
[*][b]Distribution mods[/b] (SPID, KID, FormList Manipulator, Base Object Swapper, SkyPatcher, CDF) - a tome they hand out counts as one you can find.
[*][b]powerofthree's Tweaks[/b] with "Load EditorIDs" - lets the tree group spells by their editor IDs as well as their names.
[*][b]An icon set[/b] (for example Kome's Inventory Tweaks, or Wheeler icon packs) gives the spell card its icons.
[/list]

[b]Notes[/b]
[list]
[*]The spell tome hook checks the game code before it goes in:
[list]
[*]SE 1.5.97 and AE 1.6.318 / 1.6.1170: tested in game. A check that does not match only writes a warning to the log - unless another mod has already hooked the same spot, then the hook stays out.
[*]AE 1.7.104: measured offline, not yet tested in game.
[*]AE 1.7.99: not measured. The hook goes in only if all its checks pass.
[*]Other AE 1.6.x versions: the checks are stricter than in 1.0.1, so the hook may stay out where 1.0.1 put it in.
[/list]
Wherever the hook stays out ([i]SpellLearning.log[/i] says "SpellTomeHook failed to install"), tomes work the vanilla way and the rest of the mod still runs.
[*]A tome handed out only by an SKSE plugin, or by a script that looks it up by number with no property, cannot be seen; its spell would be left out of the tree. Tell me the mod and I will look at it.
[*]Heart of Magic Librarian replaces Heart of Magic - do not run both.
[*]Crash logs: the optional [b]Debug Symbols[/b] file puts function names into Crash Logger reports. Install it only if you want to send one.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

Install with your mod manager (MO2 or Vortex) and run the installer. Then in game: open the panel (F8 by default), scan your spells, and build the tree.

[b]Coming from Heart of Magic v2.5:[/b]
[list=1]
[*][b]Disable or remove Heart of Magic first[/b] - do not install over it. The files have the same names, and your mod manager will show this as a new mod with its own version number (1.6.0 at the time of writing).
[*]Your saves, settings and saved tree carry over.
[*][b]Scan and build your tree once more[/b] to get tags, school links and load-order-safe spell IDs.
[*]If you had entered an OpenRouter key, delete [i]SKSE/Plugins/SpellLearning/openrouter_config.json[/i] and the "llm" block in [i]config.json[/i] - nothing reads them any more.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]What comes next[/b][/size]

The next goal is to connect magic mods to each other. Today every spell mod is its own island: its spells, its perks, its tomes and its ideas of fire, blood or the void live apart. The Librarian's catalog is a common language for all of them - one set of tags that any mod can read. Building on it, I want spells from different mods to grow into one another, perk and progression mods to understand spells they were never patched for, and themed magic (blood, water, wind, the void...) to find its place without hand-made lists. Requests and ideas are welcome in the Posts tab.

[line]

[size=5][b]Source code[/b][/size]

The full source (C++ plugin, PrismaUI panel, Papyrus scripts, tagging rule files, build scripts) is public at [url=https://github.com/Iwillbewarlock/HeartOfMagic]github.com/Iwillbewarlock/HeartOfMagic[/url]; the tag [i]v1.6.0[/i] is the complete source of this release (each release has its own tag), and it is also the [i]Source[/i] file on the Files tab. The project's own source is MIT. The released DLLs are GPL-3.0-or-later, as a combined work with CommonLibSSE-NG (see License below). Build it with [i]BuildRelease.ps1[/i] (see the README); CommonLibSSE-NG comes in as a git submodule (tag v10.0.0).

[line]

